  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCurrently, broad ridging is in place across the eastern U.S. with a \n5,920m 500mb high along the Carolina Coast just west of Bermuda high \npressure. A closed low is also ejecting just east of the Rockies \nwith a surface low also having developed. For our local area, this \npattern has continued the dry and warmer trend seen in the past \nseveral days to start the period. Heading into tonight, the closed \nlow will lift to the north with the surface low weakening. \nIncreasing frontogenesis is expected to the north and east following \ncontinued southerly flow from the Gulf and approaching Canadian high \npressure. This will also lead to increasing moisture. \n\nDuring the day on Friday, a downstream upper jet will move to our \nnorth with the surface front approaching the Ohio River Valley. This \nwill increase overall synoptic-scale lift with sufficient moisture \nleading to a return of rain chances, mainly north of Interstate 40. \nWith poor lapse rates below 5 C/km and instability of 250 to 500 \nJ/kg, at least isolated storms can be expected. By Saturday, another \ndownstream jet will move closer to the region, along with the \nsurface frontal boundary. By this time, significant moisture will be \nin the region with PWAT values approaching 1.8 inches, near max \nvalues for this time of year. Mid-level lapse rates are also \nexpected to rise up to 5.5 C/km. By Sunday, the front will remain \nnearby but with stronger upper divergence aloft. Given these \nindications, higher area-wide rain can be expected with locally high \namounts possible. Based on what the RRFS is showing, coverage looks \nto be higher on Sunday, in line with the upper jet strength. \nIsolated flooding remains possible, but this will depend on which \nplaces see repeated rainfall. \n\nBy Monday, the high pressure to the north will be progressing into \nthe Great Plains, pushing the frontal boundary to our south. Other \nthan some lingering chances in the south early, drier and cooler \nconditions can be expected. This will be especially true by Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning when the high is just to our north and \neast. Overall, daytime highs look close to normal in the 70s, but \novernight lows could drop well into the 40s with the radiational \ncooling.",
